# Expected differences between a test suite run under dqpass (attached to a
# queue that answers every RESOLVE with PASS) and the same run unattached
# (plan stage 1c, M9).  One extended regular expression per line, matched
# against "suite/program:case result"; matching cases are left out of the
# comparison.  Each group names the dq(4) rule that makes the difference,
# and every entry ends in its owner, "# ux-N[ D###]: cause" (the Unix-
# illusion sub-series that removes it) or "# by-design D###: reason";
# dq_oracle_test and dq_tree_suites_test fail on an entry without one.
# Every entry names its program and case, anchored:
# "^program:case[[:space:]]" (the case part may be an alternation).  A
# bare word would leave out every case whose name merely contains it and
# hide real differences there.  Both tests print each case an entry
# leaves out with the two runs' results.

# I5: no set-user-id or set-group-id transition while attached
# (P2_NO_NEW_PRIVS), no authority installation.
^procnet_cred_test:setuid_family_(root|unpriv)[[:space:]]  # by-design D137: I5, no set-id transition while attached
^procnet_exec_test:(setgid_by_unpriv|setuid_by_root|setuid_by_unpriv|setuid_clears_pdeathsig|setuid_script_ignored)[[:space:]]  # by-design D137: I5, no set-id transition while attached
^procnet_boundary_test:no_new_privs_fork[[:space:]]  # by-design D150: ATTACH sets P2_NO_NEW_PRIVS
# P2_NO_NEW_PRIVS (set at ATTACH, D150): execve_nosetid() keeps the old
# credential, so an exec does not reset the saved ids to the effective
# ones, as for any no_new_privs process.
^procnet_exec_test:saved_ids_reset[[:space:]]  # by-design D150: execve_nosetid() keeps the credential
# The cases of procnet_outside_test that attach a child of the test
# process are rows of an UNATTACHED actor.  Under dqpass the test process
# is attached: its child inherits the attachment and DQ_IOC_ATTACH refuses
# an attached process with EBUSY (dq(4), the Attaching and Inheritance
# paragraphs; kern_dqres.c, D138), so the cases skip.  Only "passed"
# (unattached) and "skipped" (attached) are left out: any other result
# of either run is compared and fails the oracle.
^procnet_outside_test:(probe_attached_target|linux_probe_attached_target|plane_services_procdesc|sigio_group_with_attached|unattached_fork_not_consulted)[[:space:]](passed|skipped)$  # by-design D138: an attached process's child is attached already, DQ_IOC_ATTACH is EBUSY (dq(4))

# I5: jail_attach(2), jail(2) with JAIL_ATTACH, and setcred(2) are refused.
^procnet_jail_test:(jail_attach_errors|jail_attach_root_escape|jail_attach_threads|jail_set_absolute|jail_set_errors|jail_set_relative)[[:space:]]  # by-design D137: I5, jail_attach(2) and JAIL_ATTACH refused
^procnet_socket_test:(fdfull_order_jail|jail_noinet)[[:space:]]  # by-design D137: I5, jail_attach(2) and JAIL_ATTACH refused
^procnet_cred_test:setcred_(root|unpriv)[[:space:]]  # by-design D137: I5, setcred(2) refused

# D153: an attached process dumps no core.
^procnet_core_test:core_(default_name|existing_file|pattern|sugid|unpriv)[[:space:]]  # ux-8 D202: an attached process dumps no core (D153)

# Design 11: kernel consumers of an attached caller's path.  md(4) looks
# its vnode up under its own locks and refuses an attached caller
# (dqres_kpath_refused); nmount(2) consults the path-valued options and
# refuses an unterminated one; unmount(2) consults the path and has no
# fallback to the literal mount-on name (D127 class 2), so its missing
# name is ENOENT, not EINVAL (also the unmount rows of
# errno_libc_and_syscall and of ktrace_sysret's KTR_SYSRET records).
^procnet_admin_test:(md_attach_vnode|swap_md_vnode)[[:space:]]  # by-design D127: md(4) looks its vnode up under its locks, dqres_kpath_refused EPERM
^procnet_admin_test:nmount_unterminated[[:space:]]  # ux-8 D207: an unterminated path option is EINVAL for an attached caller
^procnet_admin_test:unmount_fallback[[:space:]]  # ux-8 D207: no literal mount-on fallback for an attached caller
^procnet_syscall_test:(errno_libc_and_syscall|ktrace_sysret)[[:space:]]  # ux-8 D207: their unmount rows, ENOENT not EINVAL

# D155: an attached socketpair(2) tests its room for two descriptors
# before soattach, so with a full table it is EMFILE where the
# protocol's attach error (raw IP unprivileged: EPERM) came first.
^procnet_socket_test:fdfull_unchanged[[:space:]]  # ux-8 D209: attached socketpair(2) room test before soattach (D155)
